Q('l03', mk.long({ marks: 10, stem: r`The expressions $2x - 1$, $x + 1$ and $x + 3$ are consecutive terms of a geometric progression. Show all working.
(a) Find the two possible values of $x$, and write the three terms for each.
(b) For the value of $x$ that gives positive terms, find the sum of the first $10$ terms of the progression.
(c) For the other value of $x$, find the $6$th term and the sum of the first $6$ terms of the progression.`, model: J([`**(a)** The square of the middle term equals the product of its neighbours: ${wl03.md[0]}.`, `${wl03.md[1]}, so ${wl03.md[2]}, ${wl03.md[3]} and ${wl03.md[4]}.`, `For $x = 1$ the terms are $1, 2, 4$ (ratio $2$); for $x = -4$ they are $-9, -3, -1$ (ratio $\\dfrac{1}{3}$).`, `**(b)** $a = 1$, $r = 2$: ${wl03p.md[0]}.`, `**(c)** $a = -9$, $r = \\dfrac{1}{3}$: ${wl03n.md[0]}.`, `${wl03n.md[1]}.`]), sol: 'Part (a) squares the middle term and solves a quadratic; check that each root gives a common ratio. Parts (b) and (c) use the sum formula that suits each ratio.', scheme: [['(a) (x + 1)² = (2x − 1)(x + 3)', 1], ['(a) x = 1 or −4', 2], ['(a) both sets of terms', 1], ['(b) S₁₀ = 1023', 3], ['(c) T₆ = −1/27', 1], ['(c) S₆ = −364/27', 2]], d: 4, c: 'analysis', t: [1, 2], time: 720 }), (C) => { wl03.verify(C); wl03p.verify(C); wl03n.verify(C); C.assert(ratioOf([1, 2, 4]).eq(2) && ratioOf([-9, -3, -1]).eq(q(1, 3)) && sols03.every(({ x }) => ratioOf([x.mul(2).sub(1), x.add(1), x.add(3)])), 'both roots give a GP'); C.model([1023, r`-\dfrac{1}{27}`, r`-\dfrac{364}{27}`]); });
