Q('l05', mk.long({ marks: 10, stem: r`In an imagined laboratory, culture P starts with $400$ cells, and the number of cells doubles every $3$ hours. Show all working.
(a) How many cells does P have after $15$ hours?
(b) After how many hours does P first have more than $100000$ cells? (Count only whole $3$-hour periods.)
(c) Culture Q starts at the same time with $6400$ cells, and its number halves every $3$ hours. After how many hours do P and Q have equal numbers of cells, and what is that number?`, model: J([`**(a)** The counts after $0, 1, 2, \\ldots$ periods of $3$ hours form a geometric progression with $a = 400$ and $r = 2$. $15$ hours is $5$ periods: ${wl05.md[0]}.`, `**(b)** After $k$ periods the count is $400 \\times 2^{k}$. ${wl05.md[1]} and ${wl05.md[2]}, so P first passes $100000$ after $8$ periods, that is ${wl05.md[3]} hours.`, `**(c)** After $k$ periods: ${wl05k.md[0]}.`, `Multiplying both sides by $\\dfrac{2^{k}}{400}$: ${wl05k.md[1]}, so ${wl05k.md[2]} and ${wl05k.md[3]}.`, `That is after ${wl05c.md[0]} hours, when P has ${wl05c.md[1]} cells and Q has ${wl05c.md[2]} cells.`]), sol: 'Count three-hour periods, not hours: the counts are terms of a GP. Part (b) compares successive terms with the limit; part (c) equates the two general terms and compares powers.', scheme: [['(a) 5 periods, 400 × 2⁵', 2], ['(a) 12800', 1], ['(b) 2⁷ and 2⁸ compared', 2], ['(b) 24 hours', 1], ['(c) equation in k', 2], ['(c) 6 hours, 1600 cells', 2]], d: 4, c: 'analysis', t: [3, 1], time: 720 }), (C) => { wl05.verify(C); wl05k.verify(C); wl05c.verify(C); C.assert(gl05p.term(6).eq(12800) && gl05p.firstTerm('>', 100000) === 9 && k05.length === 1 && k05[0] === 2, 'values'); C.model([12800, 24, 6, 1600]); });
const gl06 = gp(3, 3);
const wl06 = W(uctx('a', [3]), [r`a + 3a + 9a = 39`, r`13a = 39`, r`a = 3`]);
const wl06s = W(gctx(gl06), [sumLine(gl06, 8), r`S_{5} = \dfrac{3\left(3^{5} - 1\right)}{2} = 363`, r`S_{6} = \dfrac{3\left(3^{6} - 1\right)}{2} = 1092`, r`363 < 1000 < 1092`]);
Q('l06', mk.long({ marks: 10, stem: r`A geometric progression has common ratio $3$, and the sum of its first three terms is $39$. Show all working.
(a) Find its first term, and write the first four terms.
(b) Find the sum of its first $8$ terms.
(c) Find the least number of terms whose sum is more than $1000$.`, model: J([`**(a)** The first three terms are $a$, $3a$ and $9a$: ${wl06.md[0]}, so ${wl06.md[1]} and ${wl06.md[2]}.`, `The first four terms are $${seqTex(gl06.list(4), { dots: false })}$.`, `**(b)** ${wl06s.md[0]}.`, `**(c)** ${wl06s.md[1]} and ${wl06s.md[2]}.`, `Since ${wl06s.md[3]}, the least number of terms is $6$.`]), sol: 'Part (a) writes the first three terms with the known ratio; part (b) is the sum formula; part (c) compares consecutive partial sums with 1000.', scheme: [['(a) 13a = 39', 1], ['(a) a = 3 and four terms', 2], ['(b) formula', 1], ['(b) S₈ = 9840', 2], ['(c) S₅ = 363 and S₆ = 1092', 3], ['(c) 6 terms', 1]], d: 3, c: 'application', t: [1, 2], time: 660 }), (C) => { wl06.verify(C); wl06s.verify(C); C.assert(gl06.sum(3).eq(39) && gl06.sum(8).eq(9840) && gl06.firstSumAbove(1000) === 6, 'values'); C.model([3, 81, 9840, 363, 1092, 6]); });
const gl07 = gp(5, 2);
const apSum = (n) => { let s = 0, t = 5; for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { s += t; t += 50; } return s; };
const firstDay07 = [...Array(30).keys()].map((i) => i + 1).find((n) => gl07.sum(n).gt(apSum(n)));
const wl07 = W(gctx(gl07), [termLine(gl07, 8), sumLine(gl07, 8), r`S_{8} = 1275 < \dfrac{8}{2}(2 \times 5 + 7 \times 50) = 1440`, r`S_{9} = 5\left(2^{9} - 1\right) = 2555 > \dfrac{9}{2}(2 \times 5 + 8 \times 50) = 1845`]);
Q('l07', mk.long({ marks: 10, stem: r`Kabir, an imagined student, starts a savings plan: ₹5 on day 1, ₹10 on day 2, ₹20 on day 3, and so on, doubling every day. Show all working.
(a) How much does he save on day 8?
(b) Find his total savings over the first 8 days.
(c) His sister Meher saves ₹5 on day 1 and ₹50 more each day than the day before, so her daily savings form an arithmetic progression. After how many days is Kabir's total first greater than Meher's?`, model: J([`**(a)** Kabir's daily savings form a geometric progression with $a = 5$ and $r = 2$: ${wl07.md[0]}, so he saves ₹640 on day 8.`, `**(b)** ${wl07.md[1]}, so he has saved ₹1,275.`, `**(c)** Meher's total after $n$ days is $\\dfrac{n}{2}\\left(2 \\times 5 + (n - 1) \\times 50\\right)$.`, `After 8 days: ${wl07.md[2]}, so Kabir is still behind.`, `After 9 days: ${wl07.md[3]}.`, 'On day 1 the totals are equal and from day 2 to day 8 Meher is ahead, so Kabir’s total is first greater after 9 days.']), sol: 'Parts (a) and (b) are the general term and sum of a GP; part (c) compares the GP total with the AP total day by day near the crossing.', scheme: [['(a) T₈ = 5 × 2⁷', 2], ['(a) ₹640', 1], ['(b) S₈ = 5(2⁸ − 1)', 2], ['(b) ₹1,275', 1], ['(c) Meher’s totals 1440 and 1845', 2], ['(c) 9 days', 2]], d: 3, c: 'analysis', t: [3, 2], time: 660 }), (C) => { wl07.verify(C); C.assert(apSum(8) === 1440 && apSum(9) === 1845 && firstDay07 === 9 && gl07.sum(1).eq(apSum(1)), 'AP totals and crossing day'); C.model([640, 1275, 1440, 2555, 1845, 9]); });
